  "articleBody": "Deciding whether to take out your existing bathtub or preserve it during a bathroom upgrade comes down to daily functionality, household accessibility, and the condition of the fixture.\n\nHow to Compare Your Options\n\n- Tub Removal for a Walk-In Shower: Tearing out an unused tub to build a walk-in shower opens up floor space, improves daily accessibility, and eliminates a high step-over barrier. It is an ideal solution for primary bathrooms or households planning for aging in place.\n- Keeping or Replacing the Existing Tub: Retaining a tub is often practical if you have young children, pets, or only one full bathroom in the home. If the tub body is sound and clean, keeping it in place also eliminates demolition costs, subfloor adjustments, and plumbing reconfigurations.\n- Plumbing and Framing Impact: Removing a tub requires reconfiguring the drain line, because shower drains typically require a larger diameter waste pipe than standard tub drains. Keeping the tub allows you to keep existing rough-in plumbing untouched.\n- Demolition Complexity: Pulling an acrylic or fiberglass tub is relatively straightforward, while removing an antique cast iron tub demands heavy labor, wall clearance, and extra disposal planning.\n\nWhat to Do Next\n\nConsider how your household actually uses the room before making structural changes. If you are ready to remove an outdated tub or convert your space in the Denver area, call Abraham Becerra at HH Remodeling LLC at 303-505-0866.",
  "datePublished": "2026-10-23"
}